POSIX condition variables are not the same as Win32 Event objects ( or dispatcher objects ) they are not even close:-)

BTW reason condition variables are better than just plain semaphores being that semaphores can just count up to a max and not
have an arbitrarily complex condition that it checks. If we just used mutex or locks then we would have to keep polling for the
value and compare it ourselves atomically.

Heres how condition variables work:

sleeper() {

  lock(m_lock)

	if (some_condition and this can be as complex as you want) {
	   wait(m_cond, m_lock); // Point A
	}

	// Point B

	do whatever to the condition you want

  unlock(m_lock)
}



waker() {
	lock(m_lock)

	if (some_condition_that_is_good_to_wakeup_sleeperthread_is_true) {
	   signal(m_cond); // Point C
	}

	// Point D

	unlock(m_lock) 
}


Point A: 
Yes you might be surprised by the fact that we are sleeping inside a lock but thats alrite. that wait does 
trap into the kernel acquire the dispatcher lock move the current thread away from the ready queue and into the blocked 
queue and in addition add it to a list of threads blocked on the condition variable m_cond. Its usually a 
HashMap<ConditionVar, List<Threads>>. Next it will release the lock m_lock internally place an acquire m_lock statement
in the instruction queue for this thread and move on to releasing the dispatcher lock. ( thats the Solaris 10 implementation )

Point B:
Fact that we re here implies that the condition has occurred and this thread has been woken up and its acquired m_lock again.
At this point this thread is free to modify the condition state if it so feels like. I think this gives it an excellent chance
to look at the state right after it was reached and before any other thread gets to modify it again.

Point C:
The signal will cause a dispatcher lock acquire and remove *some* or all threads ( based on if its signal or signal_broadcast )
that are sleeping on m_cond. 


Point D:
In the POSIX implementation, this is a noop though according to Hoare at this point this thread should block probably by acquiring
the lock again ( assuming its a fair lock ) and the thread woken up gets to execute. Hansen modified this to suggest that
the signal should be the last thing the waker does therefore this logic doesnt have to be built into the condition variable 
implementation.


Questions:
Q: Why cant we do lock(mutex) if (cond == true) do what you have to do ; unlock(mutex)
A: That works when cond == true but when cond == false what do you do? Do you keep polling? It makes no sense
   since the other threads cannot change cond therefore nobody can make it true.

Q: But what about event variables? Can't I do what makes cond == true then setEvent. Whoever is waiting for the event
   to be true will sleep on the event?
A: Yes this works but only if the thread waking up on cond == true doesnt modify the cond itself? If it does then it has
   to acquire the lock which will increase lock contesting. Condvariables take care of that for you.

Q: Not quite, the thread waking up doesnt have to recheck the condition since the event has happened. Therefore there is
   no question of acquiring any lock. Sleep when woken up assume the condition is true and proceed.
A: Oh no, events have "happened_alteast_once" semantics. There is no guarantee that the condition is still true. If what you are
   looking for is an English "event" aka something thats happened in the past and thats sufficient for you then yes use an event
   variable which is easy to implement using condition variables.

   But if what you are looking for is a condition variable, meaning the condition is true when you are woken up then events
   wont do atleast not unless they are accompanied with locks.